<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Council of Europe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1949">1949</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> is an international organisation founded in 1949 by the Treaty of London to uphold human rights, democracy and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rule-of-law">rule of law</a> in Europe. Based in Strasbourg, it is distinct from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> and has 46 member states. Its best-known instrument is the European Convention on Human Rights of 1950, enforced by the European Court of Human Rights.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/council-of-europe.jpg" alt="Council of Europe" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Architect: Henry Bernard Photo: Council of Europe · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Council_of_Europe_Palais_de_l%27Europe_aerial_view.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The French Foreign Minister Aristide Briand floated the idea of a federal union of European nations in 1929. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a> first publicly suggested a Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in a BBC radio broadcast on 21 March 1943, while the war was still being fought, and returned to the idea in a speech at the University of Zurich on 19 September 1946.</p><p>The Council was founded on 5 May 1949 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, Ireland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and the United Kingdom. Greece and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> joined three months later. Its first meetings were held in Strasbourg's University Palace, and it soon moved into its own buildings in the city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In its early years the founders drafted the European Convention on Human Rights, drawing in part on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/universal-declaration-of-human-rights">Universal Declaration of Human Rights</a>, signed only months earlier. Where the Universal Declaration was aspirational, the Convention provided an enforcement mechanism, an international court, to judge alleged violations and hold governments to account.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a> became a full member in 1951 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> joined in 1956. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, suspended in 1967 after a military coup, withdrew and was readmitted in 1974. Portugal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> joined in 1976 and 1977 after their transitions to democracy. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolutions-of-1989">revolutions of 1989</a> and the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> joined in 1990 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 1991, followed by most states of Eastern and South-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>.</p><p>The Council cannot make binding laws, unlike the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a>. It works through treaties, called conventions, which set common legal standards; some are open to states outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, such as the Budapest Convention on Cybercrime. In 1955 it adopted the flag of twelve golden stars on a blue ground, and in 1972 an anthem based on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ode-to-joy">Ode to Joy</a> theme from Beethoven's ninth symphony. The European Union later adopted both.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Its two statutory bodies are the Committee of Ministers, made up of the foreign ministers of the member states, and the Parliamentary Assembly, composed of members of national parliaments. A Secretary General, elected by the Assembly for five years, heads the secretariat. English and French are the official languages.</p><p>Membership is open to any European state that accepts the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rule-of-law">rule of law</a> and the human rights and fundamental freedoms of all persons within its jurisdiction. Judgments of the European Court of Human Rights are legally binding on the state concerned, which may have to pay compensation or change its laws or practices.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Council of Europe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Andorra joined the United Nations on 28 July 1993 and the <b>Council of Europe</b> in 1994.</blockquote><a href="/empire/andorra">Andorra · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>The republic joined the United Nations on 2 March 1992 and belongs to the <b>Council of Europe</b>, the OSCE, the Commonwealth of Independent States and the Eurasian Economic Union, which it chose in 2013 over an EU association agreement before signing the Comprehensive and Enhanced Partnership Agreement with…</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenia">Armenia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>It holds diplomatic relations with 182 countries and belongs to 38 international organisations, among them the United Nations, the <b>Council of Europe</b> since 2001, the OSCE and NATO's Partnership for Peace; it was a founder of GUAM, the Commonwealth of Independent States and the OPCW.</blockquote><a href="/empire/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Germany maintains 227 diplomatic missions and relations with more than 190 countries, belongs to the <b>Council of Europe</b>, NATO, the OECD, the G7, the G20, the World Bank and the IMF, has kept a strong alliance with France and all its neighbours since 1990 and is widely considered…</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-germany">Federal Republic of Germany · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>It joined the <b>Council of Europe</b> and GUAM, sought NATO membership from 2004, boosted its troops in the Iraq coalition, and received George W. Bush, the first sitting American president to visit.</blockquote><a href="/empire/georgia">Georgia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Greece describes special relationships with Cyprus, Italy, France, Armenia, Australia, Israel, the United States and the United Kingdom, belongs to the <b>Council of Europe</b>, the Union for the Mediterranean, NATO and the Francophonie, and joined the Three Seas Initiative in 2023.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hellenic-republic">Hellenic Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
